<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chavda dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">746 CE – 942 CE (approx.) · capital Anahilavada</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty traditionally said to have founded Anahilavada Patan, the medieval capital of Gujarat.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> chronicles of the 13th and 14th centuries tell how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanraj-chavda">Vanaraja</a>, a prince whose father had been killed in battle, was raised under the protection of a Jain monk and founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilavada</a>, modern Patan in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, around 746. Contemporary records of the dynasty are almost entirely lacking.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The chronicles portray the Chavdas as patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, a tradition that shaped later Gujarati culture. They probably ruled a small kingdom in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, at times under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a>. Inscriptions mention Chapotkata chiefs in the region, which suggests a historical basis for the chronicles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to tradition, the last king, Samantasimha, was killed by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mularaja">Mularaja</a> around 942, who founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilavada</a> the capital of a far larger kingdom.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chavda-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chavda dynasty" width="320" height="156"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gujuras_of_Sindh_Circa_AD_570-712.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>746 CE – 942 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Anahilavada</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vanaraja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Apabhramsha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chapotkata dynasty, Chavdas of Anahilavada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=844&amp;focus=chavda-dynasty" aria-label="Chavda dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6822 1336 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chavda dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7142 1592h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7142 1592h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=844&amp;focus=chavda-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
